<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Slope Fields</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's consider the autonomous differential equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac 12( y - 4).
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Make a plot of <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> versus <m>y</m>.
          Looking at the graph, for what values of <m>y</m> does
          <m>y</m> increase and for what values does it decrease?
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-1.xml"/>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-1-sol.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          <m>dy/dt = 0</m> when <m>y = 4</m>.  The quantity
          <m>y</m> increases when <m>y \lt 4</m> and decreases
          when <m>y \gt 4</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now sketch the slope field for this differential equation.
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-2.xml"/>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-2-field.xml"/>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the solutions that satisfy <m>y(0) = 0</m>,
          <m>y(0) = 2</m>, <m>y(0) = 4</m>, and <m>y(0) = 6</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-2-sol.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          All solutions converge to the equilibrium <m>y = 4</m>
          as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Verify that <m>y(t) = 4 + 2e^{-t/2}</m> is a solution
          to the differential equation with initial value
          <m>y(0) = 6</m>.  Compare its graph to the one you
          sketched above.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          <m>y'(t) = -e^{-t/2}</m> and
          <md>
            -\frac12(y-4) = -\frac12(4+2e^{-t/2}-4) = -e^{-t/2}
          </md>,
          confirming it is a solution.  Also <m>y(0) = 6</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is special about the solution where <m>y(0) = 4</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          When <m>y(0) = 4</m>, we have <m>dy/dt = 0</m>, so the
          solution is the constant function <m>y = 4</m>.  This is
          an equilibrium solution.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the differential equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = t - y.
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Plot the slope field of this differential equation below.
          Then sketch the solutions corresponding to
          <m>y(0)=0</m>, <m>y(0)=1</m>, <m>y(0)=2</m>,
          <m>y(0)=-1</m>, and <m>y(0)=-2</m>.
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-4-blank.xml"/>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-4.xml"/>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What do you think the solution is when <m>y(0)=-1</m>?
          Verify that your guess is correct.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          The solution looks like <m>y = t - 1</m> so let's check:
          <m>y' = 1</m> and <m>t - y = t - (t-1) = 1</m>,
          and <m>y(0) = -1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now let's consider the autonomous differential equation
        <md>
          \frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac 12 y(y-4).
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Make a plot of <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> versus <m>y</m>.
          Looking at the graph, for what values of <m>y</m> does
          <m>y</m> increase and for what values does it decrease?
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-5.xml"/>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-5-sol.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          <m>dy/dt = 0</m> when <m>y = 0</m> or <m>y = 4</m>.
          The quantity <m>y</m> increases when
          <m>0 \lt y \lt 4</m> and decreases when <m>y \lt 0</m>
          or <m>y \gt 4</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify any constant solutions of this differential
          equation.  We call these <em>equilibrium</em> solutions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          The equilibrium solutions are <m>y = 0</m> and
          <m>y = 4</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now sketch the slope field for this differential equation.
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-5-field.xml"/>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <image width="50%">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/25-slope-5-curves.xml"/>
        </image>
        <p>
          The slope field has positive slopes for
          <m>0 \lt y \lt 4</m>, negative slopes for <m>y \lt 0</m>
          or <m>y \gt 4</m>, and zero slopes along <m>y = 0</m>
          and <m>y = 4</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the solutions with initial values
          <m>y(0) = -1, 0, 1, \ldots, 5</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          Solutions with <m>0 \lt y(0) \lt 4</m> increase toward
          <m>y = 4</m>.  Solutions with <m>y(0) \gt 4</m> decrease
          toward <m>y = 4</m>.  Solutions with <m>y(0) \lt 0</m>
          decrease without bound.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          An equilibrium solution <m>\overline{y}</m> is called
          <em>stable</em> if nearby solutions converge to
          <m>\overline{y}</m>.  This means that if the initial
          condition varies slightly from <m>\overline{y}</m>, then
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}y(t) = \overline{y}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversely, an equilibrium solution <m>\overline{y}</m>
          is called <em>unstable</em> if nearby solutions are
          pushed away from <m>\overline{y}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Using your work above, classify the equilibrium solutions
          you found as either stable or unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          The equilibrium <m>y = 4</m> is stable since nearby solutions
          converge to it from both sides.  The equilibrium
          <m>y = 0</m> is unstable because solutions with
          <m>y(0) \gt 0</m> move away from <m>y = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that <m>y(t)</m> describes the population of a
          species of living organisms and that the initial value
          <m>y(0) \gt 0</m>.  What can you say about the eventual
          fate of this population?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="25">
        <p>
          If <m>y(0) \gt 0</m>, the population will approach the
          stable equilibrium <m>y = 4</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
